      "model": "zai/glm-5.3",
      "question": "I'm worried the paper insulation in Transformer 1 at MAIN may be involved. Pull co and co2 for every sample in the available window and work out the CO2/CO ratio for each sample. Flag any sample with a ratio below 3, which suggests paper involved in a fault, or above 10, which suggests general cellulose ageing. Also give me the min, max and mean of co and co2 over the window. Tell me whether the ratio is trending up or down across the samples. If co2 is missing for a sample, skip the ratio for that sample and say so. If co2 has no data at all, report the co trend alongside oxygen instead and say that the ratio test couldn't be done.",
      "answer": "**CO2/CO ratio analysis \u2014 Transformer 1, MAIN (2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19, 5 daily samples)**\n\n| Timestamp | CO (ppm) | CO2 (ppm) | CO2/CO ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15 | 32 | 1344 | 42.0 |\n| 2024-01-16 | 30 | 1300 | 43.3 |\n| 2024-01-17 | 35 | 1350 | 38.6 |\n| 2024-01-18 | 33 | 1325 | 40.2 |\n| 2024-01-19 | 32 | 1340 | 41.9 |\n\n**Flags:**\n- No sample below 3 (no evidence of paper involved in an active fault).\n- **All 5 samples are above 10** \u2014 this is consistent with general cellulose (paper) ageing rather than a fault, as you suspected regarding the paper insulation.\n\n**Statistics over the window:**\n- CO: min 30, max 35, mean 32.0\n- CO2: min 1300, max 1350, mean 1331.8\n\n**Trend:** The ratio is roughly flat with no sustained direction \u2014 it dips mid-window (43.3 \u2192 38.6) then recovers (40.2 \u2192 41.9). Net change over the window is essentially zero (42.0 \u2192 41.9). No meaningful upward or downward trend.\n\nNo samples were skipped; CO2 was present in every sample, so the ratio test was fully completed. Overall, the data points to normal cellulose ageing rather than a paper-involved thermal fault.",
      "score": {
